{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`ticket_planner.py:74-82,192` recognizes `requires_create` and `requires_parent`, but production outbound CREATEs never carry either value. The current emission path is `_compute_outbound_create_mutation` in `outbound_mutation_builders.py:122-173`, then `outbound_pass.py:257-269`, which converts the raw `OutboundMutation` to a typed `Mutation` whose `target` is `om.local_id`. `OutboundCreatePayload` has no prerequisite fields, so metadata added only to the raw builder would be lost before the planner sees it.\n\nThe planner is not yet authoritative for CREATEs. `batch_dispatch.reroute_migrated_plans` deliberately excludes create actions, and `create_route` coordinates only one create/containment sequence. The live batch consumption point is `apply_handlers.handle_create` (with the sibling typed-create route in `apply_outbound.py`); `create_route.should_hold_dependent` currently has no production caller. Thus ordering requires a new per-pass create scheduler at the batch sequencer, not a claim that the existing single-create coordinator already orders parent and child creates.\n\n## Approach\n\nUse local ticket IDs for all prerequisite and provider identities. An outbound create has `Mutation.target == om.local_id`, which is also its `TicketPlan.identity`; a prerequisite must never name a Jira key for an unbound ticket.\n\nDerive a prerequisite only for a real local parent/create edge in `outbound_mutation_builders`, carry it on `OutboundMutation`, and model it explicitly on `OutboundCreatePayload`. Preserve it in the `outbound_pass` conversion as top-level mapping metadata visible to `ticket_planner._collect_dependencies`. `batch_dispatch._mutation_to_batch_dict` must exclude this scheduler-only metadata from Jira vendor fields.\n\nAdd a focused per-pass create-scheduling seam, owned by the batch application path, for homogeneous outbound-create `TicketPlan`s. It receives the finalized plans and their legacy batch mutations, uses the existing planner dependency order, and dispatches each selected create through the existing `apply_handlers.handle_create` → `create_route` path exactly once. It translates the selected route’s terminal result into a provider outcome: only a confirmed create whose containment releases dependents marks its local ID successful; permanent, `commit_unknown`, budget-deferred, or unconfirmed containment outcomes hold dependents as `dependency_deferred`. It records those outcomes in the existing manifest/tally shape, removes only consumed creates from the following legacy loop, and leaves mixed/non-create plans on their established paths.\n\nRefactor the batch create adapter and the typed `apply_outbound` create adapter to expose the same structured create-route completion signal (`confirmed` / `dependents_released`) without a second Jira call or a second routing selector. This keeps `create_route` the owner of one-create persistence, containment, and rollback selection while the new scheduler owns only inter-create ordering.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_differ.py` — raw `OutboundMutation` prerequisite carrier\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_mutation_builders.py` — derive parent/create prerequisite local IDs\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/outbound_pass.py` and `mutation_payloads.py` — preserve typed payload metadata and its mapping round trip\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/ticket_planner.py` — retain/document local-ID dependency and provider semantics\n- a new create-ordering sibling (or equivalent focused existing-seam extraction) plus `applier.py` — schedule eligible create plans before the legacy mutation loop and preserve manifest/tally accounting\n- `apply_handlers.py`, `create_route.py`, and `apply_outbound.py` — expose and consume the single create-route completion signal at both live create consumption points\n- `batch_dispatch.py` — batch projection and removal of scheduler-only metadata from Jira fields\n- focused outbound-payload, planner, create-scheduler, handler/route, and operation-coordinator tests\n\n## Testing\n\nExercise the full builder → typed payload → planner → per-pass create-scheduler → `handle_create`/`create_route` path with parent and child initially unbound. Cover confirmed parent success, permanent failure, `commit_unknown`, budget defer, unconfirmed containment, an already-bound parent, an unknown local parent, unrelated creates, and the vendor-field boundary. Assert local IDs—not Jira keys—at every provider/dependency edge, exactly one create-route execution per selected mutation, and no duplicate dispatch through the legacy loop.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The create builder emits a prerequisite only for a real local parent/create edge, using the parent local ticket ID and never inventing a Jira key for an unbound parent.\n- [ ] The raw `OutboundMutation`, `OutboundCreatePayload`, and `outbound_pass` conversion preserve each prerequisite as planner-visible metadata; `_mutation_to_batch_dict` excludes it from Jira vendor fields.\n- [ ] Each provider create has `Mutation.target` equal to the local ID advertised to dependents, and planner tests prove that this identity resolves without a local-ID↔Jira-key conversion.\n- [ ] The batch sequencer selects eligible homogeneous outbound-create plans, executes them in planner dependency order through `apply_handlers.handle_create` and the single selected `create_route`, then removes only those executed/deferred creates from the legacy loop.\n- [ ] A confirmed parent create releases its child only after the parent’s matching local-ID-to-Jira binding and containment are confirmed; the child is dispatched once through the same route.\n- [ ] A permanent failure, `commit_unknown`, REST-budget defer, or unconfirmed containment holds the dependent as `dependency_deferred` and makes no child vendor-create call.\n- [ ] The batch and typed create adapters expose equivalent `confirmed`/`dependents_released` completion signals without dual-send or a second route selector.\n- [ ] An already-bound parent local ID satisfies the dependency without a new parent create; an unknown local parent ID is `dependency_deferred`; unrelated creates retain their existing behavior.\n- [ ] Focused payload, planner, scheduler, handler/route, and operation-coordinator suites pass."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788378408061483001,"uuid":"5049ef5d-a4ab-492c-b6bf-711733e41acc"}